%% Optimal transport on a curved surface, told with a particle system.
%% Build:  python3 lmr2svg.py examples/optimal-transport/deck.lmr -o optimal-transport.html
!title Moving Sand on a Sphere
!subtitle Optimal transport on curved surfaces, one grain at a time
!author lemur examples

!slide[.middle] The oldest question in the field

In 1781 Gaspard **Monge** asked how to move a pile of sand into a hole of the same
volume with the **least total effort**.

!pause

Two centuries later, *optimal transport* compares images, shapes, point clouds and
probability distributions — and it works on **curved spaces** too. Here: a sphere.

!slide Two distributions of sand

!anim
	!src measures.py

!slide Monge and Kantorovich

Monge: find a map $T$ that pushes $\mu$ onto $\nu$ at the least cost,

:: math
	\min_{T_\#\mu = \nu} \int_M \mk{cost}{d\big(x, T(x)\big)^2} \, d\mu(x)

!annotate
	cost[#ef7d2d]: on a curved surface $d$ is the **geodesic** distance

!pause

Kantorovich lets mass split: a **plan** $\pi$ with marginals $\mu$ and $\nu$,

:: math
	W_2^2(\mu, \nu) = \min_{\pi \in \Pi(\mu,\nu)} \int_{M\times M} d(x,y)^2 \, d\pi(x,y)

!pause

For $N$ grains of equal mass the best plan is a **permutation** — a linear
assignment problem, solved exactly.

!slide Following the optimal map

!anim
	!src transport.py

!slide[.middle] Straight lines on a sphere

Each grain travels along a **great circle** — McCann's *displacement
interpolation* $\mu_t$ moves mass along geodesics:

:: math
	\mu_t = \big(\gamma_t\big)_\# \pi, \qquad \gamma_t(x,y) = \exp_x\!\big(t\,\log_x y\big)

!pause

That is the tangent space at work: $\log_x y$ points from $x$ towards $y$, and
$\exp_x$ walks it back onto the surface.

!slide Not every plan is a good plan

!anim
	!src compare.py

!slide[.middle] Why the optimal paths never cross

!theorem Cyclical monotonicity
	The support of an optimal plan never contains two pairs $(x_1,y_1)$,
	$(x_2,y_2)$ that would be cheaper swapped:
	:: math
		d(x_1,y_1)^2 + d(x_2,y_2)^2 \;\le\; d(x_1,y_2)^2 + d(x_2,y_1)^2

Crossing paths could always be uncrossed at a lower cost — so the optimal ones don't cross.

!slide[.middle] Sinkhorn: transport with a blur

Add entropy and the problem becomes smooth, convex, and fast — it is solved by
alternately rescaling the rows and columns of a matrix:

:: math
	\pi_\varepsilon = \operatorname*{arg\,min}_{\pi \in \Pi(\mu,\nu)} \int d^2\, d\pi \;+\; \varepsilon\,\mathrm{KL}(\pi \,\|\, \mu\otimes\nu)

!pause

The price: the plan is **blurry**. Each grain is spread over many targets, and its
average destination — the barycentre — is pulled towards the middle.

!slide From blurry to sharp

!anim
	!src sinkhorn.py

!slide[.plain]

!anim
	!src finale.py
	!viewport full

!slide[.middle] Take-aways

+ **OT** moves one distribution onto another at the least total cost
+ on a curved surface the cost is the **geodesic** distance, and mass travels along geodesics
+ optimal paths **don't cross**; random ones tangle
+ **Sinkhorn** trades exactness for speed: a blurry plan, pulled towards barycentres
